1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
One in five hundred people has this disease.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:08,470
A test catches ninety-nine percent of the sick and wrongly flags one percent of the healthy.

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:11,740
If yours says positive, what's the chance you're sick?

4
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:13,030
Pick a number.

5
00:00:14,966 --> 00:00:19,516
Most doctors and medical students given a problem like this got it wrong.

6
00:00:19,866 --> 00:00:24,966
Their most common answer was their test's own accuracy: ninety-five percent.

7
00:00:25,346 --> 00:00:29,026
For our test, that's ninety-nine: you'd start treatment today.

8
00:00:29,366 --> 00:00:31,096
Check that rule by counting.

9
00:00:31,296 --> 00:00:35,126
Here are ten thousand people, and suppose half of them are sick.

10
00:00:35,466 --> 00:00:36,596
Test everyone.

11
00:00:36,796 --> 00:00:41,026
Ninety-nine percent of the sick light up, and one percent of the healthy.

12
00:00:41,366 --> 00:00:45,186
Of the five thousand who lit up, ninety-nine percent are sick.

13
00:00:45,486 --> 00:00:46,966
Here, the rule holds.

14
00:00:47,333 --> 00:00:53,863
Now keep the same test, and make the disease rarer and rarer, down to yours: one in five hundred.

15
00:00:54,666 --> 00:01:02,436
About twenty sick people light up, plus one percent of the nine thousand nine hundred eighty healthy ones: about a hundred.

16
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,550
So of a hundred and twenty positives, only twenty are sick.

17
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:08,223
Seventeen percent.

18
00:01:09,233 --> 00:01:10,963
The rule said ninety-nine.

19
00:01:11,163 --> 00:01:12,743
Six times too high.

20
00:01:13,733 --> 00:01:18,343
One percent sounds small, but here it's one percent of nearly everyone.

21
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:23,250
Your positive result ruled out everyone who tested negative.

22
00:01:23,866 --> 00:01:28,976
What's left is a smaller sample space: these hundred and twenty people.

23
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:32,693
Your chance of being sick is the red share of this crowd.

24
00:01:32,993 --> 00:01:35,393
Twenty, out of twenty plus a hundred.

25
00:01:36,966 --> 00:01:45,656
Unrounded, the red count is twenty sick people times ninety-nine percent, and the blue, every healthy person times one percent.

26
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,300
Divide everything by the ten thousand.

27
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:53,800
Counts become shares: two in a thousand sick, nine hundred ninety-eight healthy.

28
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,390
The share can't change.

29
00:01:55,733 --> 00:02:01,203
These are how common each group is, and these, how often each group tests positive.

30
00:02:01,503 --> 00:02:03,713
Name them, and you have Bayes' theorem.

31
00:02:04,066 --> 00:02:09,356
All it does is shrink the sample space to what the evidence allows, then take the share.

32
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:12,980
Swap the disease for a police watch list.

33
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:20,523
For the twenty seventeen Champions League final, about a hundred and seventy thousand people came to Cardiff.

34
00:02:20,723 --> 00:02:28,303
The watch lists held about twelve hundred photos, so even a small error rate per face would swamp the real matches.

35
00:02:28,666 --> 00:02:31,426
Face-recognition cameras scanned the crowds.

36
00:02:31,726 --> 00:02:36,046
Over four days, they raised two thousand four hundred and seventy alerts.

37
00:02:36,433 --> 00:02:39,063
Shrink to the alerts, and take the red share.

38
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:44,010
The police counted a hundred and seventy-three as right: seven percent.

39
00:02:44,366 --> 00:02:48,716
In machine learning, the share of alerts that are right is called precision.

40
00:02:49,466 --> 00:02:55,886
Cardiff University's evaluators counted only alerts the logbooks confirmed: three percent.

41
00:02:56,266 --> 00:03:08,676
Months later, a new algorithm, better photos and practised operators thinned out the false alarms, the blue term in Bayes' denominator, and their count rose to forty-six percent.

42
00:03:09,033 --> 00:03:10,253
Back to your test.

43
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,510
Your seventeen percent assumes a random screen.

44
00:03:15,810 --> 00:03:25,850
Symptoms raise the one in five hundred, and the answer climbs: at one in a hundred, fifty percent; at one in two, ninety-nine, where the rule held.

45
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:33,720
The title's ninety-nine percent is the test's recall: how often the sick test positive.

46
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:38,840
Seventeen percent is its precision: how often the positive are sick.

